<section class="services" aria-labelledby="home-services-title">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 id="home-services-title">What I Own for Online Stores</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      E-commerce breaks at the seams &mdash; between the theme and the checkout, the catalog and the ERP,
      the ad platform and the report you actually trust. Those seams are the work.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Storefront &amp; Conversion</strong>
        Product pages, collection and search, cart and checkout. Fix the steps where revenue leaks before
        spending more to send traffic into them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Catalog &amp; Merchandising</strong>
        Product data you can trust, variants that make sense, and merchandising rules that hold up when the
        catalog doubles. Clean data upstream is why everything downstream works.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Operations &amp; Integrations</strong>
        Orders, inventory, fulfillment, returns, and the connections to your ERP, 3PL, and accounting.
        Fewer manual exports, fewer surprises at month end.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Platform Strategy &amp; Replatforming</strong>
        Straight answers on whether to stay, extend, or move &mdash; and a migration plan that protects
        your URLs, your rankings, and your order history when the answer is move.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Analytics &amp; Lifecycle</strong>
        Tracking that survives a checkout change, attribution you can defend in a meeting, and email and
        retention flows measured on repeat revenue instead of open rates.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

